INT. WAX MUSEUM - GRAND GALLERY - NIGHT

Hushed. Rows of figures in frozen poses: a queen, a boxer, a saint, a pirate. Their glass eyes catch the sodium glow from a security lamp.

HAROLD WINTERBOTTOM, sixties, night guard, sits on a stool with a thermos. Near the pirate, cleaners ZUZANA KRAJEWSKI, forties, and MOSES TEMBO, thirties, sweep. Apprentice KAI NAKAMURA-REID, nineteen, polishes a cabinet. Curator ILSE ARENDT, fifties, taps a tablet by the door.

HAROLD
Quiet night.

ILSE
They're always quiet.

ZUZANA
(low)
Moses. The boxer moved.

MOSES
He did not.

ZUZANA
His fist was down.

MOSES
It's still down.

Kai stops polishing. He looks at the boxer's fist. It is raised.

KAI
Ms. Arendt?

ILSE
Don't be silly, Kai.

The lights dip. Harold stands, torch in hand, the stool toppling. Ilse's tablet blinks out. Zuzana and Moses back toward the saint, brooms raised, and Kai edges to Harold's side, polishing cloth clutched in his fist like a talisman.

The door at the far end creaks open.

VESPER LANE, thirties, in a long grey coat and white gloves, steps in. Pale. Perfectly still. Hair pinned. A lace veil tucked into her collar. She stops beside the queen. She tilts her head and looks at the five living people the way someone looks at figures in a window.

VESPER
(softly)
I'm so sorry I'm late.

HAROLD
We're closed, miss.

VESPER
I know. I'm the fiftieth figure.

She walks to an empty plinth near the pirate and steps onto it. Turns. Folds her hands. And stops, mid-breath, mid-smile, mid-blink.

No one moves. The boxer's fist drops with a soft clack.
